Tonsillitis
Opthalmology & ENT
Enlarged erythematous tonsils, Tonsillar exudate, Severe odynophagia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-046
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe throat pain preventing oral intake, high fever, and fear of airway closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young adult with sudden onset of severe bilateral odynophagia, high-grade fever, and a muffled voice, completely unable to swallow oral fluids. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to rule out impending airway obstruction (kissing tonsils) or peritonsillar abscess (Quinsy), and to initiate immediate systemic antibiotic and analgesic therapy to prevent severe dehydration and hospital admission prior to the next morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My throat is completely swollen shut, it hurts to even swallow my own spit, and I'm burning up."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced rapidly over the last 24 hours. Patient reports a severe, acute sore throat prominently featuring severe bilateral odynophagia. Notes high-grade fever, systemic malaise, and generalized body aches. Complains of a muffled, thick voice quality and pronounced difficulty swallowing solid foods and fluids, frequently leading to pooled oral secretions and minor drooling. Endorses referred bilateral otalgia (ear pain). Family notes extreme halitosis directly related to the throat infection.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly fatigued, and spitting saliva into a tissue to avoid painful swallowing.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living with a roommate; significant risk of severe dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 | Temp: 39.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Flushed, diaphoretic, toxic-appearing, speaking with a thick, muffled voice.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals bilaterally enlarged, swollen, and intensely erythematous palatine tonsils visibly meeting in the midline (kissing tonsils). Thick, confluent, yellowish-white purulent exudate is strictly confined to the tonsillar crypts and surfaces. The uvula is midline and not edematous. Tender, notably enlarged jugulodigastric and anterior cervical lymph nodes are palpable bilaterally. Airway is patent without stridor. Bilateral tympanic membranes are clear (referred otalgia).
Environmental Observations: Overheated room; patient shivering under multiple heavy blankets.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Acute Bilateral Tonsillitis (Suspected Bacterial) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Peritonsillar Abscess (Quinsy) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symmetric, bilateral enlargement of the tonsils without unilateral soft palate fluctuance or uvular deviation, and the absence of severe trismus.

Infectious Mononucleosis (Glandular Fever) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyper-acute 24-hour onset (mono is typically insidious over weeks), absence of generalized posterior cervical lymphadenopathy, and lack of hepatosplenomegaly.

Epiglottitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The direct visualization of massive tonsillar hypertrophy entirely explaining the dysphagia and muffled voice, without the classic tripod positioning, stridor, or severe respiratory distress of supraglottic inflammation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Roommate present to monitor hydration)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook case of hyper-acute, severe bilateral tonsillitis with "kissing tonsils" causing partial oropharyngeal obstruction and fluid refusal. Urgent direct clinical evaluation was critical to secure the airway, definitively rule out a peritonsillar abscess requiring surgical drainage, and manage the intense fever (39.4C). Because the patient was deteriorating rapidly and unable to tolerate standard oral analgesia due to severe odynophagia, immediate bedside intervention with targeted, easily swallowed liquid/dispersible formulations was required to break the fever cycle and prevent an emergency department admission for intravenous rehydration during the after-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Phenoxymethylpenicillin 500mg QID for 10 days. Administered Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1g STAT via crushed/dispersible formulation to bypass solid dysphagia.
Positive Behavioral Change: To maintain hydration and mechanically soothe the severe inflammation, the patient must eat one entire icy-pole (popsicle) every 2 hours while awake. The extreme cold provides instant, localized topical anesthesia to the inflamed tonsils (positive reinforcement), completely overriding the pain response of swallowing and ensuring a continuous, slow drip of much-needed hydration and calories.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to ensure reduction in tonsil size; if 'kissing tonsils' persist or worsen, consider ED referral for IV steroids/antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient begins to make a harsh whistling sound when breathing in (stridor), if they absolutely cannot swallow saliva and begin to choke, or if they cannot open their mouth more than a finger's width.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; swab sent for confirmation of Streptococcus.
Clinical Course: The agonizing throat pain and high fever will begin to break within 24-48 hours of starting the penicillin. The massively swollen tonsils will take several days to shrink.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your breathing becomes noisy, or if your jaw suddenly locks shut and you cannot open your mouth to drink, you must call an ambulance immediately.
PATIENT EDUCATION

